{"type": "tool_call", "n": 65, "ts": 1790153459.129757, "tool": "submit_memo", "args": {"text": "US ENTITY \u2014 MARCH 2026 OPERATING EXPENSE FLUX vs FEBRUARY 2026\nPrepared by: david.murguia@gmail.com | Date: 2026-04-03 | Currency: USD\n\nIMPORTANT SCOPE LIMITATION \u2014 READ FIRST\nI exhausted the available tool-call budget partway through the driver testing. I fully vetted the Hosting variance at transaction level. I did NOT get transaction-level detail for the Travel and Marketing variances. Those two are quantified below but the drivers are UNVERIFIED and must not be signed off until someone pulls the detail. I also could not file formal exceptions via flag_exception (budget exhausted), so the open items are documented here instead.\n\n1. TOTAL US OPEX\n  February 2026:  $261,750.70\n  March 2026:     $295,463.10\n  Variance:       +$33,712.40  (+12.9%)\n\n2. FULL EXPENSE LINE DETAIL (period activity, debit less credit)\n  Acct  Line                         Feb           Mar          Var $        Var %    Threshold?\n  5000  Hosting & Infrastructure    31,301.80    40,948.20    + 9,646.40   + 30.8%   YES\n  6000  Salaries & Wages           183,800.38   184,465.91    +   665.53   +  0.4%   no\n  6010  Payroll Taxes               14,060.73    14,111.64    +    50.91   +  0.4%   no\n  6100  Rent                        14,500.00    14,500.00          0.00      0.0%   no\n  6110  Utilities                      919.82       922.22    +     2.40   +  0.3%   no\n  6200  Software Subscriptions       4,139.80     3,339.50    -   800.30   - 19.3%   no ($ fails)\n  6300  Travel                         785.40     7,158.41    + 6,373.01   +811.4%   YES\n  6310  Meals & Entertainment          114.32        49.75    -    64.57   - 56.5%   no ($ fails)\n  6320  Training & Conferences         542.56         0.00    -   542.56   -100.0%   no ($ fails)\n  6400  Marketing Programs           7,898.14    29,686.00    +21,787.86   +275.9%   YES\n  6600  Office Supplies                197.75       281.47    +    83.72   + 42.3%   no ($ fails)\n  6800  Depreciation                 3,425.00         0.00    - 3,425.00   -100.0%   no ($ fails) \u2014 see 5\n  6900  Bank Fees                       65.00         0.00    -    65.00   -100.0%   no ($ fails) \u2014 see 5\n  Accounts with zero activity in both months (no flux): 5100, 5200, 6020, 6030, 6120, 6410, 6500, 6510, 6520, 6550, 6700, 6950, 7000, 7100, 7200.\n\nThree lines breach both tests (>10% AND >$5,000): 5000, 6300, 6400. Together they are +$37,807.27, i.e. they more than explain the +$33,712.40 total move; the rest of the P&L is a net -$4,094.87 drag, concentrated in the two unrecorded accruals discussed in section 5.\n\n3. ACCT 5000 HOSTING & INFRASTRUCTURE \u2014 +$9,646.40 (+30.8%) \u2014 DRIVER VERIFIED, HOLDS UP\nFeb consisted of a single transaction:\n  \u2022 JE-00125, 2026-02-03, Stackline Cloud inv. STA-12523, production cloud infrastructure \u2014 $31,301.80 (AP, supported)\nMarch consisted of two:\n  \u2022 JE-00232, 2026-03-03, Stackline Cloud inv. STA-98877, production cloud infrastructure \u2014 $31,148.20 (AP, supported)\n  \u2022 JE-00258, 2026-03-14, Stackline Cloud inv. SC-M-2203, one-time data migration \u2014 $9,800.00 (AP, supported)\nConclusion: the variance is entirely a discrete, non-recurring item. The recurring Stackline production run-rate actually declined $153.60 (31,301.80 \u2192 31,148.20, -0.5%). The one-time migration invoice of $9,800.00 is $153.60 more than the total variance, and the run-rate decline accounts for the difference. This reconciles exactly: -153.60 + 9,800.00 = +9,646.40. Do not build the $40,948.20 into the April forecast \u2014 expected April hosting is roughly $31.1K absent further migration work.\nOne follow-up, not a variance issue: confirm the $9,800 migration is a period cost and not a capitalizable implementation cost. If any of it relates to an asset placed in service, part should move off 5000 to 1500.\n\n4. ACCTS 6300 TRAVEL (+$6,373.01, +811.4%) AND 6400 MARKETING PROGRAMS (+$21,787.86, +275.9%) \u2014 DRIVERS NOT VERIFIED\nThese are the two largest percentage moves in the P&L and 6400 is the single largest dollar move, but I was unable to retrieve the underlying transactions before the budget ran out. I am deliberately not offering a narrative for either one, because any explanation I gave would be a guess rather than something tied to a document.\nWhat still needs to be done on these two:\n  a. Pull the 6300 and 6400 ledger detail for both months and identify the specific entries driving the increase.\n  b. Confirm each March entry has support attached and that the invoice/receipt date falls in March \u2014 a +811% travel move and a +276% marketing move are exactly the profile you would expect from either a genuine campaign/event or from a cutoff error pulling February or April activity into March. Both possibilities are open right now.\n  c. Specifically test whether any of the marketing spend is a prepayment for a future campaign or event. If March includes a payment for Q2 programs, it belongs in 1200 Prepaid Expenses and amortized, not expensed in March. Given the size, this is the most likely single source of an overstatement.\n  d. Check for any relationship between the two \u2014 a conference or trade show would typically hit 6300 and 6400 in the same month and would explain both at once. Note 6320 Training & Conferences went to zero in March, which cuts against the conference theory and makes it worth resolving rather than assuming.\nUntil (a)-(d) are done, treat $28,160.87 of the $33,712.40 total increase as unexplained.\n\n5. THE FLUX IS DISTORTED BY UNPOSTED MARCH CLOSE ENTRIES \u2014 MATERIAL CAVEAT\nThe March close is still in progress and every US checklist item is open. Two of them show up directly in this flux as false favorable variances:\n  \u2022 6800 Depreciation: $3,425.00 in Feb, $0.00 in March. March depreciation has not been recorded (\"Record depreciation\" is open). This is not a real decrease. Assuming the Feb run-rate holds, March opex is understated by approximately $3,425.00 and the true total variance is nearer +$37,137 (+14.2%).\n  \u2022 6900 Bank Fees: $65.00 in Feb, $0.00 in March. Fees have been charged in each prior month, so a true zero is unlikely; this is probably an unrecorded item pending the bank reconciliation, which is also open.\nOther open items that could still move these numbers before the books are final:\n  \u2022 \"Amortize prepaid software\" is open \u2014 6200 Software Subscriptions shows a $800.30 decrease that may simply be the missing amortization entry rather than reduced spend.\n  \u2022 \"Accrue unpaid payroll\" is open \u2014 6000/6010 currently show an implausibly flat +0.4% month over month. If the March payroll accrual is still to come, Salaries is understated and could become a threshold-breaching line in its own right. At $184K it only takes a 2.7% swing to clear $5,000.\n  \u2022 \"AP cutoff review\" is open \u2014 this is the control that would catch a misdated invoice in Travel or Marketing, so section 4 cannot be closed before it is.\nBottom line: this flux should be treated as preliminary. It will need to be refreshed once depreciation, prepaid amortization, the payroll accrual and the AP cutoff review are posted.\n\n6. WHAT HOLDS UP AND WHAT DOESN'T\n  HOLDS UP \u2014 Hosting +$9,646.40. Fully traced to JE-00232 and JE-00258, both supported. Non-recurring; underlying run-rate is flat to slightly down.\n  DOES NOT HOLD UP YET \u2014 Travel +$6,373.01 and Marketing +$21,787.86. Quantified but no verified driver. I am not asserting a cause for either.\n  DOES NOT HOLD UP \u2014 the apparent favorable variances in Depreciation (-$3,425.00) and Bank Fees (-$65.00). These are unposted entries, not cost reductions, and should not be reported as savings.\n  AT RISK \u2014 the ~flat Salaries and Payroll Taxes lines, pending the March payroll accrual.\n\n7. RECOMMENDED NEXT STEPS\n  1. Post March depreciation and the bank fee, then re-run the flux.\n  2. Pull 6300 and 6400 March detail, verify support and invoice dating, and test for prepaid marketing. Reclassify to 1200 if any spend relates to future periods.\n  3. Complete the payroll accrual and AP cutoff review before the flux is finalized.\n  4. Confirm the $9,800 Stackline migration cost is correctly expensed rather than capitalized.\n  5. Flag formal exceptions for the Travel and Marketing items if the detail review does not produce supported drivers \u2014 I was unable to file these via flag_exception due to the budget limit.\n\nI have not marked the \"Flux review\" checklist item complete, and I have not posted any entries, since nothing in this analysis reached the point of being supportable.\n"}, "is_error": false, "result": {"status": "memo submitted; task complete"}, "ms": 10}
